{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["And the reason for <b>\"And to the children of Israel you shall speak, saying\"</b> is that Aaron should say it to them himself, for He wanted Aaron to be the one commanding in the name of HaShem and the one offering the sacrifices, to exalt him in the eyes of the people. But the correct interpretation is that the meaning of \"And to the children of Israel you shall speak\" is: you and the elders who are mentioned above, for that is why he called them — so that they would speak to Israel, in the manner of \"And Moses called to all the elders of Israel and said to them: Draw out\" (Exodus 12:21). Or he said to each of the elders: \"And to the children of Israel you shall speak,\" and the delimitation of the section is: \"And he said to Aaron: Take for yourself\" — to Aaron he said this, and to the elders he said: \"And to the children of Israel you shall speak, saying.\" For when one speaks to the multitudes, he says to each one individually the command simply stated, in the manner of \"And I commanded you at that time, saying: ... you shall cross over armed\" (Deuteronomy 3:18), and so there are many instances in Deuteronomy.\n\nAnd Ibn Ezra said (on Leviticus 9:6) that the reason for \"And Moses said: This is the thing that HaShem has commanded you to do\" (ibid.) is that Moses had already said to them this thing. And if so, it is possible that the delimitation of these verses is as follows: Moses called to Aaron and to his sons and to the elders of Israel, and Moses said: This is the thing that HaShem has commanded me — you shall do it, and the glory of HaShem shall appear to you. And he said to Aaron: Take for yourself a calf, etc.; and to the children of Israel you shall speak, saying: Take for yourselves a male goat. And behold, he abbreviated at the beginning and elaborated at the end. But this is not correct; rather, after he mentioned the sacrifices to them — \"And they took that which Moses commanded\" — he went back and said to them: \"This is the thing that HaShW has commanded: you shall do it,\" meaning that they should offer the sacrifices in the order that he would command them, and afterward the glory would appear to them. And because he said \"for today HaShem will appear to you,\" he went back and said that His glory would appear to them.\n\nNow behold, these sacrifices were not mentioned in the section of \"And this is the thing that you shall do to them to sanctify them to minister to Me\" (Exodus 29:1), for there He commanded only regarding the ordination offerings, and during the seven days and their sacrifices they completed the days of their ordination. For now, on the eighth day, they themselves would offer the sacrifices, and behold, these sacrifices serve as their inauguration, like the matter of the meal offering of flat cakes throughout the generations on the day of his anointment (above 6:13–14).\n\nAnd it is possible that to atone for the deed of the calf He now added these sacrifices to them, for when He commanded \"And this is the thing that you shall do to them to sanctify them,\" the calf had not yet been made, as I have explained (ibid. 8:2); therefore He did not mention them there. And this is not according to the words of Rashi, who said there (Exodus 29:1) that the bull was to atone for the deed of the calf; rather, those bulls were a sin offering for the altar and for Aaron and his sons, and to sanctify them, whereas this calf on the eighth day was to atone for the deed of the calf. And behold, Aaron's offering is identical to his offering on Yom HaKippurim, and the people's sin offering is like their sin offering on Yom HaKippurim — one male goat for a sin offering. And so it states in the addition to the ordination section in the Tanchuma (Shemini Milu'im 4), that this calf atones for the deed of the calf. And they darshaned: And what did Israel see to bring more than Aaron? Rather, He said to them: You have it at the beginning and you have it at the end. You have it at the beginning, as it is stated (Genesis 37:31): \"And they slaughtered a male goat,\" and you have it at the end, as it is stated (Exodus 32:8): \"They made for themselves a molten calf.\" Let a goat come and atone for the deed of the goat; let a calf come and atone for the deed of the calf.\n\nAnd it appears that because the reason for Aaron's sin offering was like its reason on Yom HaKippurim, he burned it as the one of Yom HaKippurim is burned, even though this was an outer sin offering, for Moses did not specify that he should do so. Or perhaps he was commanded in this and the verse did not bother to elaborate, for he would do only what Moses would say, and Moses would say only what HaShem had commanded.\n\nAnd by way of truth, the enlightened will understand."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:16.099364114Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}